          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Major Works</strong><br>\nSophocles' most famous works are the three plays about Oedipus: \"Oedipus Rex,\" \"Oedipus at Colonus,\" and \"Antigone.\" These plays are considered his masterpieces and are central to the canon of Greek tragedy. They deal with themes of fate, free will, and the struggle against the inevitability of destiny.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Style of Writing</strong><br>\nSophocles' writing style was characterized by its clarity, directness, and lucidity. His use of dramatic irony, where the audience knows more than the characters on stage, created a powerful emotional effect. Sophocles was also known for his deep philosophical insights and the ability to portray complex human emotions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Drama</strong><br>\nThe work of Sophocles greatly influenced the development of drama in Western culture. His plays have been performed continuously for over 2,500 years and are still relevant to contemporary audiences. His approaches to storytelling and stagecraft laid the foundation for future generations of playwrights.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
