          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Religious and Political Life</strong><br>\nSophocles was actively involved in the religious and political life of Athens. He served as a treasurer responsible for managing imperial revenues and as a general, a rare honor for a playwright. His involvement in these areas suggests that he was a respected citizen with a role in shaping the culture and politics of his city.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nSophocles was married to Nicostrata, with whom he had two sons, Iophon and Sophocles the Younger. His work had a profound effect on later literature, philosophy, and psychoanalysis, particularly the concept of the Oedipus complex introduced by Sigmund Freud. His legacy endures as a pinnacle of classical literature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nSophocles died in 406 BCE, but the exact cause of his death is unknown. In his lifetime, he was already celebrated for his work, having won numerous competitions in Athenian festivals. After his death, his plays continued to win awards when they were performed, a testament to his enduring brilliance as a playwright.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
